Și, mai ales, nu vă grăbiți. Am avut un elev care în clasa a treia nu terminase nicio carte și care în clasa a șasea citea sub bancă în timpul orei mele, ceea ce m-a supărat exact atât cât trebuia și m-a bucurat mult mai mult. Între cele două momente nu se întâmplase nimic spectaculos. Cineva îi dăduse, la momentul potrivit, cartea potrivită, iar cineva, probabil o mamă obosită, continuase să-i citească seara, când nimeni nu mai credea că are rost.

Are rost. Zece minute, o carte deschisă, un copil lângă tine. Nu e o metodă. E, cred eu, una dintre cele mai simple și mai durabile forme de dragoste pe care le avem la îndemână. Și, spre deosebire de aproape orice altceva din creșterea unui copil, nu costă aproape nimic și nu poate fi făcută greșit.$demo$), now() - interval '5 days'
where not exists (select 1 from public.chapters where chapters.id = '00000000-0000-4000-9100-002700030000'::uuid);

-- demo-28: "The Quarry Lake in Winter" by adult_writer (Robert reads this one)
insert into public.stories (id, user_id, slug, title, blurb, language_code, category_id,
  cover_kind, cover_design, cover_background, cover_accent, rating, ai_disclosure,
  first_published_at, last_chapter_published_at, created_at)
select '00000000-0000-4000-9100-002800000000'::uuid, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001'::uuid, 'the-quarry-lake-in-winter-demo-28',
  $demo$The Quarry Lake in Winter$demo$, $demo$Nell left Caddow at eighteen and never really came back. Now her brother Tam has drowned in the flooded quarry, drunk, in January, and the town has already decided whose fault it was. Three days in a cold house with a mother who won't say his name, a notebook of unfinished plans and a lake that gives nothing back.$demo$, 'en',
  (select id from public.categories where slug = 'fiction'),
  'drawn', 'twotone', 'plum', 'gold', '16_plus', 'no_ai',
  now() - interval '47 days', now() - interval '41 days', now() - interval '49 days'
where not exists (select 1 from public.stories where stories.id = '00000000-0000-4000-9100-002800000000'::uuid);
select public.set_story_tags('00000000-0000-4000-9100-002800000000'::uuid, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001'::uuid, array['family', 'grief', 'small town', 'winter', 'sisters and brothers']);
update public.stories
set cover_kind = 'drawn', cover_design = 'twotone', cover_background = 'plum', cover_accent = 'gold'
where stories.id = '00000000-0000-4000-9100-002800000000'::uuid
  and (cover_kind, cover_design, cover_background, cover_accent)
    is distinct from ('drawn', 'twotone', 'plum', 'gold');
insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, user_id, code, position, draft_title, draft_body, draft_version,
  draft_saved_at, published_title, published_body, status, first_published_at, last_published_at, word_count,
  published_word_count, created_at)
select '00000000-0000-4000-9100-002800010000'::uuid, '00000000-0000-4000-9100-002800000000'::uuid, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001'::uuid,
  'd028c001', 1, $demo$Chapter 1: The Kitchen Light$demo$, public.check_chapter_text($demo$The taxi from the station wouldn't go further than the war memorial, so Nell carried her bag the last half mile up Quarry Road the way she had carried her school bag at eleven, leaning into the wind off the moor. Nothing had moved. The chip shop had a new sign and the same smell. The Methodist chapel was a carpet warehouse now, which somebody had told her years ago, and she had said "oh" and forgotten it.

Her mother's kitchen light was on. It was always on. Growing up, Nell had thought of it as a lighthouse, and later, in the city, as something more like a surveillance camera.

Her mother opened the door before she knocked.

"You'll want tea," Jean said, and turned back inside, and that was the greeting.

The kitchen was exactly itself: the brown teapot, the calendar from the feed merchant, the radio turned low so it was company without being conversation. Tam's boots were by the back door. Nell looked at them once and then at the kettle.

"When did they..." she started.

"Tuesday. The divers." Jean set two mugs down with great care, as if they might go off. "They've done what they do. The undertaker's got him. Thursday, eleven o'clock, St Mark's, then the Crown after. Dougie Hale's sorting the Crown."

"Dougie."

"He was Tam's friend. One of them."

Nell wrapped her hands round the mug. Her mother had put the milk in first, the way Nell had stopped doing at nineteen because a flatmate had laughed at it.

"Mum. How was he? Before."

Jean looked at the window. The dark pressed on the glass. "He was Tam."

That was all she would get, Nell understood. Her mother had a way of closing a subject like a drawer, with a small firm push and no sound, and you could stand there with your fingers in it or you could step back.

She stepped back. She asked about the hens, which were fine, and about Auntie Marion's hip, which was not, and at half past nine her mother said she'd made up Nell's old bed and went upstairs without saying goodnight, because she never had.

Nell sat in the kitchen for a long time. On the fridge, under a magnet shaped like a sheep, was a list in Tam's square capitals: BULBS. GATE HINGE. CAR TAX. RING NELL. The last one had no tick against it. None of them did.

She did not cry. She washed the mugs, dried them, put them back on the wrong hooks, and left the kitchen light on.$demo$), 1,
